CM_ BO_ 2183149062 "[confirmed] The headlight. Identified by remote command: the console logged HLON at
1788785214.368 and HLOFF at 1788785234.208, and this message changed 0.77 s and
0.33 s later respectively. Nothing else on the bus changed in either window
except the uptime counter ticking.";
CM_ SG_ 2183149062 HeadLightOn "[confirmed] Byte 1. Goes to 1 within 0.77 s of the HLON command and to 0 within 0.33 s of
HLOFF, then to 1 again 2.0 s after UNLOCK and to 0 1.3 s after LOCK -- so
unlocking the bike switches the headlight on, which is why the periodic GTFRI
report still showed head_light_status=1 after HLOFF. Cross-checks against
<ECU Info> field 5 (Head Light Status) in the same session.";
CM_ SG_ 2183149062 BusAwake "[confirmed] Byte 0. 1 = the bike is awake, 2 = asleep. Bytes 3 and 5 carry the same fact
inverted: b0=1 goes with b3=b5=1, and b0=2 with b3=b5=0, in every one of the
311 frames of the three 7 September sessions. Bytes 0, 3 and 5 are one signal.

CORRECTION. This byte was previously recorded as \"HeadLightSource\", on the
reading that 0x02 meant the change came from an explicit HLON/HLOFF command
and 0x01 from the unlock sequence. That was wrong, and the 4 September corpus
disproves it on its own: that recording contains no UNLOCK at all, yet byte 0
is 2 in all 103 frames while byte 1 still toggles the headlight on and off.
Byte 0 says nothing about who asked.

What it does track is the lock. Against the HubLock's own state on 13B76400 it
agrees in 60/61, 116/120 and 115/120 frames across the three sessions, the
disagreements all being frames inside a transition. It is 2 throughout the
4 September corpus, which is a bike that was never unlocked.

WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THIS MESSAGE. Unlocking the bike wakes the bus: 5
identifiers become 94. This byte is that state, published at a lamp's address,
which is why the headlight appeared to switch on by itself after UNLOCK.";
CM_ BO_ 2182616582 "[confirmed] The rear light. Same method as the headlight: RLONEN at 1788785392.364 and
RLOFF at 1788785412.125, with this message changing 0.76 s and 0.40 s later,
and nothing else changing in either window.";
CM_ SG_ 2182616582 RearLightOn "[confirmed] Byte 4. Goes to 1 within 0.76 s of RLONEN and to 0 within 0.40 s of RLOFF, and
also tracks UNLOCK (0.28 s) and LOCK (0.52 s). Cross-checks against <ECU Info>
field 6 (Rear Light Status).";
CM_ SG_ 2182616582 MaxSpeed "[confirmed] CORRECTION -- this signal was called RearLightAwakeLevel and read as a
brightness percentage. It is not a light signal at all. It is the configured
maximum assisted speed, the same value 03FF1600 byte 7 carries, mirrored at
the rear light's address.

CONFIRMED -- by the same six commands, in the same session, as 03FF1600. Byte 2
followed each one:

    UNLOCK       0100120001030200   byte 2 = 18
    GTECC:19     0100130001030200   byte 2 = 19
    GTECC:25     0100190001030200   byte 2 = 25
    GTECC:15     01000F0001030200   byte 2 = 15
    GTECC:10     01000A0001030200   byte 2 = 10
    GTECC:5      0100050001030200   byte 2 =  5
    GTECC:25     0100190001030200   byte 2 = 25
    LOCK         0000000000030200   byte 2 =  0

A search over every identifier and every byte position for the commanded
sequence [18, 19, 25, 15, 10, 5, 25] returned exactly two hits: this byte and
03FF1600 byte 7. Nothing else on the bus carries the value.

WHY THE OLD READING WAS REASONABLE, AND WHAT ACTUALLY DISPROVED IT. The
previous note observed correctly that byte 2 held 25 while awake and 0 while
asleep, and reasoned that 25 in a byte with only two values looked like a
percentage. Both halves were sound. The trap was that 25 km/h is the EU
pedelec limit, so the factory value of a configuration byte is indistinguishable
from a constant until somebody reconfigures it -- and the 0 when asleep is the
ECU not publishing, not a measured zero.

The test the old note proposed -- command RLONEN on an unlocked bike and see
where byte 2 lands -- would NOT have caught this. Byte 2 does not respond to
the lamp at any lock state. What caught it was changing the thing itself.

The 0 while asleep is now read the same way as every other zeroed ECU field:
absence of data, not a measurement. See nodes.md.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DISTURB. Byte 0 (BusAwake) and byte 4 (RearLightOn) are
unaffected and stay confirmed. The observation that separated them from byte 2
in the first place -- with the bike locked, RLONEN turns byte 4 on while byte 2
stays 0 -- still holds and now has a plainer explanation: byte 4 is the lamp
and byte 2 was never about the lamp.";
CM_ SG_ 2182616582 BusAwake "[confirmed] Byte 0. 1 = the bike is awake, 0 = asleep. The same fact that byte 0 of
02203606 carries, in the same sense as the lock rather than inverted.

Agrees with the HubLock's own state on 13B76400 in 99/100 and 115/120 frames,
and is 0 throughout the 4 September corpus, which is a bike that was never
unlocked. It is 0 through the RLONEN window of the 7 September session too,
while byte 4 turns the lamp on -- which is what separates the two signals.";
CM_ BO_ 2478269440 "[confirmed] The lock (immobiliser) reporting its state. A single-byte message, and the only
one on the bus with priority 0x13.

Identified across two independent sessions by driving UNLOCK and LOCK remotely
and watching which byte tracked the resulting state. Method: build the lock
state timeline from the +RESP:GTMLS reports, then look for bytes whose value
sets are disjoint between locked and unlocked intervals. Exactly one byte
qualified across 2 locked and 2 unlocked intervals.

CONFIRMED BY DISCONNECTION: a third session was recorded with the HubLock
disabled. This identifier went from 139 frames to zero, and it was the ONLY
identifier to disappear -- 95 identifiers became 94, with none appearing in its
place. Both lamps still toggled normally in that session, so the bus was
healthy and the remote commands were still being processed. The component that
sends this is therefore the HubLock, and AA=0x64 is its address.

NOT the battery lock. <ECU Info> field 13 (Battery-Lock State) stayed 0 through
all three sessions while this byte flipped.";
CM_ SG_ 2478269440 Unlocked "[confirmed] Byte 0. 1 = unlocked, 0 = locked -- note this is the inverse of GTFRI's
ECU Lock State field, so it reads as an \"unlocked\" flag. Named for what the
raw value means rather than inverting it here.

Six transitions across two sessions, every one following its command:
  UNLOCK -> 1 after 2.12 s, 2.55 s, 2.17 s
  LOCK   -> 0 after 0.32 s, 0.46 s, 0.58 s

CORRECTION -- what the asymmetry measures. This was recorded as evidence of a
motorised latch, on the grounds that unlocking takes about four times as long
as locking. That reading does not survive: the whole bus shows the same
asymmetry. 04FF3604, the 30 ms message, starts 2.1-2.6 s after each UNLOCK and
stops 0.3-0.8 s after each LOCK, in all four unlock and three lock events
across the three 7 September sessions. So the 2.1 s is the wake sequence
bringing 89 identifiers up, and this byte is simply reported at the end of it.
The asymmetry still argues against a command echo, but it says nothing about
the latch.

Repeated UNLOCK commands on an already-unlocked bike produce no transition, so
this byte reports actual state and not command receipt. That is why session 2's
six UNLOCK commands yielded only two unlock transitions.

DO NOT use GTFRI's ECU Lock State as a substitute for this signal. With the
HubLock disabled and no 13B76400 on the bus at all, that field still reported
lock_state flipping 0 and 1, so it tracks what the IoT module believes rather
than what the lock did.";
CM_ BO_ 2214532608 "[confirmed] The configured maximum assisted speed. Published at 1 Hz while the bike is
awake and absent entirely while it is locked -- in the unlock/lock session the
first frame arrives 0.8 s after each UNLOCK and the last one lands before each
LOCK.

Only byte 7 was ever seen to move. The rest held 14 2B 00 01 04 00 13 across
every session on record, through six speed changes, a lock cycle and a session
boundary.";
CM_ SG_ 2214532608 MaxSpeed "[confirmed] CONFIRMED -- tied to a commanded action, six times in one session. The
set-speed-while-unlocked session sent six AT+GTECC commands to an awake bike
and byte 7 followed every one of them, on the CAN log's own clock:

    command      byte 7    latency
    (at open)        18    carried in from the previous session
    GTECC:19         19    1.39 s
    GTECC:25         25    1.26 s
    GTECC:15         15    0.89 s
    GTECC:10         10    1.21 s
    GTECC:5           5    0.81 s
    GTECC:25         25    1.29 s

Six commanded values, six matches, no misses and no intermediate values. The
message runs at 1 Hz, so a latency of 0.8-1.4 s is one period plus the command
round trip -- there is no room in that for the byte to have reached the value
any other way.

CONFIRMED -- the scale, as 1:1 km/h with no offset, over 5 to 25. Six distinct
commanded values is enough to fix a linear scale outright rather than infer it.

CONFIRMED -- that it persists. The session opened at 18, which is where the
previous session's last GTECC left it, across an intervening lock and a gap in
recording. So this is stored configuration and not a transient.

WHY 25 LOOKED LIKE SOMETHING ELSE FOR SO LONG. Every session before the first
GTECC read 25, because 25 km/h is the EU pedelec limit and nobody had changed
it. A configuration byte nobody has reconfigured is indistinguishable from a
constant. See the correction on 02181606 MaxSpeed, which is this same value
mirrored at the rear light's address and was read as a brightness percentage
for exactly this reason.

UNKNOWN -- byte 6, which holds 19 and did not move through any of the six
commands. A second limit of some kind is the obvious guess and there is no
evidence for it. Note that 19 is also a value GTECC was given, which makes it
easy to misread a coincidence as a match; byte 6 held 19 both before and after
that command.

TO DISPROVE. Command a value outside the 5-25 range tested here -- 0, or 40 --
and see whether byte 7 follows, clamps, or the command is refused. A clamp
would say the ECU bounds what it accepts, which the range tested cannot show.";
CM_ BO_ 2248099330 "[confirmed] BMS cell voltages, cells 1-4. Confirmed without guesswork: the +RESP:GTBMI
report relays this frame's payload verbatim -- the identical byte string
1410191019101910 appears both in the CAN payload and in the report -- and
GTBMI states a cell count of 13, which is exactly what 05FF4602 through
05FF4605 supply (4+4+4+1). The 13 cells sum to 53.622 V against the 53.696 V
that GTFRI reported for the pack at the same moment, a 0.14 % difference.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099330 Cell4 "[confirmed] Little-endian uint16 in mV.";
