CM_ BO_ 2248099333 "[confirmed] BMS cell voltage, cell 13, then padding. This pack has 13 cells, so the last
frame of the series carries one value and six zero bytes -- which is how the
cell count was established.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099333 Cell13 "[confirmed] Little-endian uint16 in mV. Bytes 2-7 are always zero: only 13 cells.";
CM_ BO_ 2248099334 "[probable] BMS temperatures. Payload 1C1C1C reads as 28, 28, 28 which is a plausible cell
temperature in degrees Celsius, and the same byte string is relayed in
+RESP:GTBMI immediately after the cell voltage frames. Which physical sensor
each byte is has not been established: <ECU Info> field 12 lists cell maximum,
cell minimum, MOS and \"other\" temperatures, and there are more of those than
there are bytes here.

DO NOT read these six bytes as \"the cell temperatures\", and in particular do
not take their maximum as the pack's cell maximum. Across the three
7 September sessions <ECU Info>'s Cell Temperature Max changes within a
session -- 29,29,28,28,28 in the first, 28,28,28,29,29,29 in the second --
while all six bytes of 05FF4606 and 05FF4607 hold still. No byte of either
frame is the cell maximum. See 05FF4607.Temp5 for the one sensor that can be
ruled out positively.

The whole corpus spans 27 to 29 degC, so there is not enough range here to
assign the bytes at all. What would settle it: warm one part of the pack --
a charge cycle heats the MOS, a discharge heats the cells -- and see which
bytes move together.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099334 Temp3 "[probable] 0x1C = 28 degC. Sensor identity unknown.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099334 Temp2 "[probable] 0x1C = 28 degC. Sensor identity unknown.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099334 Temp1 "[probable] 0x1C = 28 degC. Sensor identity unknown; the scale may also have an offset.";
CM_ BO_ 2248099335 "[probable] More BMS temperatures: 1D1B1C reads as 29, 27, 28 degC, and is likewise
relayed verbatim in +RESP:GTBMI. The spread across the two frames is 27-29 degC
on a stationary bike at room temperature, which is what makes the reading
plausible.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099335 Temp6 "[probable] 0x1C = 28 degC. Sensor identity unknown.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099335 Temp5 "[probable] 0x1B = 27 degC, and the one temperature byte whose identity can be settled by
elimination: it is NOT a cell sensor.

In every @Track report of every session <ECU Info> gives Cell Temperature Min
as 28 degC. This byte reads 27. A pack cannot have a cell colder than its
reported cell minimum, so whatever this sensor measures, the BMS does not
count it as a cell. It is not the MOS or \"other\" temperature either -- both
are reported as 28 or 29 and this byte matched neither in any of the 21
comparable reports.

Ambient, board or inlet temperature are what is left. Untested.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099335 Temp4 "[probable] 0x1D = 29 degC. Sensor identity unknown.";
CM_ BO_ 2248099344 "[confirmed] The BMS reporting the pack terminals: voltage in bytes 0-3, current in bytes
4-7, both big-endian 32-bit, both at 273 ms. This was the \"273 ms, bytes 3 and
7 varying, at the BMS address, not part of the GTBMI relay\" entry in nodes.md.

Two independent confirmations, which is why this is the best-established
message on the bus after the cell voltages themselves:

1. The current bytes appear VERBATIM in the serial console. The last four
   bytes of <Battery Status> in +RESP:GTFRI are the pack current, and 27 of
   the 29 reports across the three 7 September sessions carry byte-for-byte
   the same value as the nearest frame of this message -- FFFFFFA9, FFFFFF9E,
   FFFFFFCA and so on, matched to within 0.1 s. The two that differ are
   adjacent samples of a value that changes every 273 ms.

2. The voltage agrees with a signal confirmed by a different route. Summing
   the 13 cells of 05FF4602-05FF4605 gives the same number to within 2 mV on
   96.9 %, 97.1 % and 98.7 % of the three sessions' frames (693 in total),
   worst case 6 mV. The two are measured and transmitted separately, so they
   are not the same reading twice, and the residual is the sampling offset
   between a 273 ms message and a 1044 ms one.

Note the voltage here reads 73-93 mV BELOW what +RESP:GTFRI gives as
<Main Power Voltage> (median 84 mV over 22 comparable reports). That is expected: GTFRI's figure is the tracker
measuring its own supply rail, which is why GTFRI still reports a pack voltage
on a locked bike with the BMS asleep and this message absent. Do not treat the
two as interchangeable.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099344 PackCurrent "[confirmed] Bytes 4-7, big-endian, SIGNED, mA. 0xFFFFFFA9 is -87 mA, not 4294967209.

The sign is not an inference. Every frame in all three sessions reads negative
(-32 to -104 mA), which is what a parked bike does: a small standing draw. The
same bytes reach the serial console unchanged, where the @Track parser already
treats the field as two's complement for the same reason.

Sign convention: negative is discharge. Nothing recorded so far charges the
pack, so the positive direction is untested. A charge cycle would settle it.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099344 PackVoltage "[confirmed] Bytes 0-3, big-endian, mV. 0x0000D176 = 53622 mV, which is exactly the sum of
the 13 cell voltages at the same moment. Bytes 0-1 have been zero in all 693
frames recorded, so the field could be 16 bits; 32 is written here because the
current field beside it demonstrably needs all four of its bytes for the sign.
Observed range 53562-53631 mV over three sessions.";
CM_ BO_ 2248099337 "[confirmed] State of charge and state of health. The BMS sends this unsolicited at 1044 ms
while the bike is awake; 05124609 is the identical payload returned when the
IoT module polls for it, which it does every 300 s even while the bike sleeps.
That poll is how the tracker can report a battery percentage on a locked bike,
and the pair 02294609 -> 05124609 is one of only five identifiers present on a
sleeping bus.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099337 StateOfHealth "[confirmed] Bytes 4-5, big-endian, 0.1 %. Matches <Battery Status> group 2, which the
@Track parser calls battery_health_pct:

  4 September   0x03E8 = 1000 -> 100.0 %   serial 100
  7 September   0x03CA =  970 ->  97.0 %   serial 97

Two values three days apart, both matching. A drop from 100 to 97 in three
days is not real degradation -- these estimates jump after a charge -- but the
agreement is what matters here.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099337 StateOfCharge "[confirmed] Bytes 2-3, big-endian, 0.1 %. Confirmed against <Scooter Battery Percentage>
in +RESP:GTFRI at four different values across the whole corpus:

  4 September   0x0370 =  880 ->  88.0 %   serial 88
  7 Sep, s1     0x03E8 = 1000 -> 100.0 %   serial 100
  7 Sep, s1     0x03DE =  990 ->  99.0 %   serial 99
  7 Sep, s2/s3  0x03D4 =  980 ->  98.0 %   serial 98

The step from 1000 to 990 is the strongest part. It happens on the bus at
t=918 s of the first session, and the serial's own reports go from 100 to 99
between t=914 s and t=1155 s -- so the CAN value changes inside the gap
between the two reports that bracket it. The bus leads the console, which is
the right way round for the bus being the source.

A 12-point range settles the scale. The serial reports whole percent, so the
0.1 resolution comes from this field being ten times the reported number in
every one of the four cases.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099337 BmsReportMode "[hypothesis] Byte 0 is 1 or 3. It is 1 on every 300 s poll reply while the bike is asleep
and 3 within a second or two of each UNLOCK, so it looks like the same
awake/asleep distinction that bytes 0 of 02203606 and 02181606 carry. Not
tested against anything else, and 1 and 3 is an odd pair for a boolean, so
this may be a mode with values not yet seen.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567305 "[confirmed] The reply to the IoT module's 02294609 poll, with the same layout as
05FF4609. Sent every 300 s, and one of the five identifiers that survive on a
sleeping bus -- so this frame, not the unsolicited one, is what the tracker
actually reports from most of the time.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567305 StateOfHealth "[confirmed] As 05FF4609.StateOfHealth.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567305 StateOfCharge "[confirmed] As 05FF4609.StateOfCharge; this is the frame the 88/99/100 % readings came from.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567297 "[hypothesis] Reply to 02294601. Payload 007300000000, identical to the unsolicited 05FF4601
and to the first block +RESP:GTBMI relays (0073000000000000, zero-extended to
eight). Constant in every frame ever recorded, so there is nothing to fit and
no signals are defined.

Byte 1 = 0x73 with everything else zero has the shape of a flag word -- GTBMI
places it where a protection/status field would sit -- but a constant is
consistent with any reading. What would test it: a pack fault, or a second
pack.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567298 "[confirmed] Reply to 02294602. Cells 1-4, as 05FF4602. Relayed verbatim in +RESP:GTBMI.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567298 Cell4 "[confirmed] As 05FF4602.Cell4.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567298 Cell3 "[confirmed] As 05FF4602.Cell3.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567298 Cell2 "[confirmed] As 05FF4602.Cell2.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567298 Cell1 "[confirmed] As 05FF4602.Cell1. 0B10 reads as 0x100B = 4107 mV.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567299 "[confirmed] Reply to 02294603. Cells 5-8, as 05FF4603. Relayed verbatim in +RESP:GTBMI.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567299 Cell8 "[confirmed] As 05FF4603.Cell8.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567299 Cell7 "[confirmed] As 05FF4603.Cell7.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567299 Cell6 "[confirmed] As 05FF4603.Cell6.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567299 Cell5 "[confirmed] As 05FF4603.Cell5.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567300 "[confirmed] Reply to 02294604. Cells 9-12, as 05FF4604. Relayed verbatim in +RESP:GTBMI.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567300 Cell12 "[confirmed] As 05FF4604.Cell12.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567300 Cell11 "[confirmed] As 05FF4604.Cell11.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567300 Cell10 "[confirmed] As 05FF4604.Cell10.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567300 Cell9 "[confirmed] As 05FF4604.Cell9.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567301 "[confirmed] Reply to 02294605. Cell 13, as 05FF4605. Bytes 2-7 are padding.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567301 Cell13 "[confirmed] As 05FF4605.Cell13.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567302 "[probable] Reply to 02294606. As 05FF4606: 1E1E1D reads as 30, 30, 29 degC. Which sensor
each byte is remains unassigned -- see 05FF4606 for why, and for the warning
against reading their maximum as the pack's cell maximum.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567302 Temp3 "[probable] As 05FF4606.Temp3.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567302 Temp2 "[probable] As 05FF4606.Temp2.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567302 Temp1 "[probable] As 05FF4606.Temp1.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567303 "[probable] Reply to 02294607. As 05FF4607: 1E1D1D reads as 30, 29, 29 degC.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567303 Temp6 "[probable] As 05FF4607.Temp6.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567303 Temp5 "[probable] As 05FF4607.Temp5.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567303 Temp4 "[probable] As 05FF4607.Temp4.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567312 "[confirmed] Reply to 02294610. The pack terminals, same layout as 05FF4610: voltage in
bytes 0-3, current in bytes 4-7, both big-endian 32-bit, the current signed.

Only four frames exist, all from the battery-removal session, and all four
decode against the serial console:

  rel  98.76  0000D0E3FFFFFFC4  53475 mV, -60 mA  |  GTFRI: 53.546 V, -60 mA
  rel 170.08  0000D0E3FFFFFFCA  53475 mV, -54 mA  |  GTFRI: 53.546 V, -54 mA
  rel 217.02  0000D0E3FFFFFFCA  53475 mV, -54 mA  |  GTFRI: 53.546 V, -60 mA
  rel 257.11  0000D0E1FFFFFFCF  53473 mV, -49 mA  |  GTFRI: 53.546 V, -54 mA

The current matches exactly in two of four; the other two are 3 s away from
their report, and the pack current moves every 273 ms. The voltage sits 71 mV
below GTFRI's <Main Power Voltage>, which is the same offset 05FF4610 shows
and for the same reason: GTFRI measures the tracker's own supply rail.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567312 PackCurrent "[confirmed] As 05FF4610.PackCurrent -- big-endian, SIGNED, mA. 0xFFFFFFC4 is -60 mA, and
+RESP:GTFRI reported exactly -60 mA 2.8 s earlier.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567312 PackVoltage "[confirmed] As 05FF4610.PackVoltage. 0x0000D0E3 = 53475 mV.";
CM_ BO_ 2298049792 "[probable] Reply to 02417500, in a request/reply family that had never been seen:
02 41 75 xx -> 08 F9 75 xx. Both appeared for the first time in the
7 September battery-removal session, 13 frames each, only during the sweep the
master runs when the pack is connected.

AA = 75 is a component the bus had never revealed. Under the nodes.md scheme
this is component 7, channel 5, and channel 5 is the identity channel at every
other address: BB = 00 hardware version, BB = 01 firmware version. The payload
shape agrees -- a single 32-bit value in bytes 0-3 with bytes 4-7 zero, exactly
like 03FF1500, 04F93500 and 05124500.

It is \"probable\" and not \"confirmed\" because <ECU Info> carries only three
version groups and all three are already accounted for by AA = 15, 35 and 45.
This component's version string reaches the serial console nowhere, so there
is no ground truth to check it against. What would settle it: a component
whose firmware version is known independently, or the spec PDF in reference/.";
CM_ SG_ 2298049792 HardwareVersion "[probable] Bytes 0-3 = 0x000000F4 = 244, constant across all 13 frames.";
CM_ BO_ 2298049793 "[probable] Reply to 02417501. See 08F97500 for the family and the caveat.";
CM_ SG_ 2298049793 FirmwareVersion "[probable] Bytes 0-3 = 0x00000015 = 21, constant across all 13 frames.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567316 "[confirmed] Highest and lowest cell, with which cell each one is, and the spread between
them. Returned on request (02294614) during the wake-up enumeration.

Confirmed by internal consistency against a signal established independently:
all 8 frames recorded across the three 7 September sessions agree exactly with
the maximum and minimum of the 13 cell voltages decoded from 05FF4602-4605 at
the same moment -- both values, BOTH INDICES, and the spread:

  10220B101400000E   4130 mV @ 11,  4116 mV @ 0,  spread 14
  101F0B101100000E   4127 mV @ 11,  4113 mV @ 0,  spread 14
  101D0B101000000D   4125 mV @ 11,  4112 mV @ 0,  spread 13

Eight frames, five fields each, no misses. Two indices matching by chance
across three sessions is not a plausible coincidence, and the values move
between sessions, so this is not a constant agreeing with a constant.

WORTH NOTING: the voltages here are BIG-endian, while the cell voltages in
05FF4602-4605 are little-endian. The same device uses both. Read big-endian,
0x1022 = 4130 mV, a credible cell; read little-endian it would be 8720 mV,
which is impossible, so the endianness is not a matter of taste here.

Cell indices are 0-based: cell 1 of the pack is index 0.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567316 CellVoltageSpread "[confirmed] Bytes 6-7 big-endian. Equal to MaxCellVoltage - MinCellVoltage in all 8
frames: 14, 14, 13 mV. Redundant with the two fields above, which is what
makes it a useful check that the frame has been read correctly.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567316 MinCellIndex "[confirmed] Byte 5. Always 0, and cell index 0 was in fact the lowest in all 8 frames.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567316 MinCellVoltage "[confirmed] Bytes 3-4 big-endian. 4116, 4114, 4113 and 4112 mV, each the true minimum.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567316 MaxCellIndex "[confirmed] Byte 2. Always 11 so far, and cell index 11 was in fact the highest in all 8 frames.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567316 MaxCellVoltage "[confirmed] Bytes 0-1 big-endian. 4130, 4127 and 4125 mV, each the true maximum of the 13 cells.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567313 "[confirmed] Pack capacity: how much charge the pack holds when full, and how much is left.
Returned on request (02294611) during the wake-up enumeration.

Confirmed by arithmetic against a signal confirmed by another route. Remaining
divided by full-charge, rounded to whole percent, is the state of charge that
05FF4609 reports and that the serial console repeats:

  13771 / 13964 = 98.62 %  ->  99   and SOC read 990
  13747 / 13964 = 98.45 %  ->  98   and SOC read 980
  13736 / 13964 = 98.37 %  ->  98   and SOC read 980

Remaining also falls monotonically across the three sessions, in the same
order and over the same hours as the pack voltage and the state of charge.

Bytes 0-1 hold 97, which is the state of health the serial console reports,
so the same number appears here and in 05FF4609 -- consistent, and a useful
cross-check on the frame.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567313 RemainingCapacity "[confirmed] Bytes 4-7 big-endian, mAh. 13771 -> 13747 -> 13736 across the three sessions,
falling monotonically. Divided by FullChargeCapacity it reproduces the
reported state of charge in every session.

Bytes 4-5 have always been zero. 32 bits is written here because the field
sits in the four bytes the frame has left; a pack this size will never fill
more than 16 of them.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567313 FullChargeCapacity "[confirmed] Bytes 2-3 big-endian, mAh. 0x368C = 13964, unchanged in all three sessions,
which is what a full-charge estimate should be over three hours.

Consistency: 13964 at 97 % health implies a design capacity near 14400 mAh,
which is a round number for a pack whose own model string is RP13S35A
(13 cells in series -- see 05124502). That is arithmetic, not evidence; the
confirmation is the SOC division in the message comment.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567313 StateOfHealth "[confirmed] Bytes 0-1 big-endian, whole percent. 0x0061 = 97, matching <Battery Status>
battery_health_pct in all three sessions. Note the units differ from
05FF4609.StateOfHealth, which carries the same quantity in 0.1 %.";
CM_ BO_ 2248099328 "[probable] Payload 0D0300900303, unchanged in every session. Byte 0 is 0x0D = 13, the
number of cells -- which +RESP:GTBMI states independently and which
05FF4602-05FF4605 supply exactly (4+4+4+1). The remaining five bytes are
0x03, 0x0090, 0x03, 0x03 and are not decoded; a pack configuration of some
kind, but nothing in the corpus varies them, so nothing can be fitted.";
CM_ SG_ 2248099328 CellCount "[probable] Byte 0 = 13. Agrees with the cell count +RESP:GTBMI reports and with the
number of cell voltage values on the bus. Still \"probable\" rather than
confirmed because a constant agreeing with a constant is weak: a pack with a
different cell count would settle it, and there is only one pack.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567317 "[probable] Highest and lowest temperature with sensor indices, by exact analogy with
05124614's treatment of the cell voltages. Returned on request (02294615).

  4 September onwards, s1     1C011C0100xx   28 @ 1, 28 @ 1
  7 September, s2 and s3      1D011C0301xx   29 @ 1, 28 @ 3

The two readings match <ECU Info>'s Cell Temperature Max and Min in the second
and third sessions (29 and 28) and in three of the five reports of the first.
The structural parallel with 05124614 -- value, index, value, index -- is what
makes the layout probable rather than a guess.

Not confirmed, and it will not be from this corpus: every temperature anywhere
in it is 27, 28 or 29 degC, so a field that reads 28 agrees with almost
anything. The last two bytes vary between 0x0010/0x00FF and 0x0100/0x01FF and
are not decoded.

Note the sensor indices here are 1-based, unlike the 0-based cell indices in
05124614. That is not a typo: index 1 picks out the byte that carries the
matching value in 05FF4606 only under 1-based numbering.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567317 MinTempIndex "[hypothesis] Byte 3. 1 in the first session, 3 in the other two. Moves, which is why it looks like an index.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567317 MinTemp "[probable] Byte 2. 28 degC in all three sessions, matching Cell Temperature Min.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567317 MaxTempIndex "[hypothesis] Byte 1. Always 1. A sensor index by analogy with 05124614; nothing has moved it.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567317 MaxTemp "[probable] Byte 0. 28 degC in the first session, 29 in the other two, matching Cell Temperature Max.";
CM_ BO_ 2214532352 "[confirmed] Reply to 02191500. 0x000000C3 = group 1 of <ECU Info> <Hardware Version>, verbatim.";
CM_ SG_ 2214532352 HardwareVersion "[confirmed] Bytes 0-3 = 0x000000C3. Appears as the first group of the serial's hardware version.";
CM_ BO_ 2214532353 "[confirmed] Reply to 02191501. 0x0000004A = group 1 of <ECU Info> <Firmware Version>, verbatim.";
CM_ SG_ 2214532353 FirmwareVersion "[confirmed] Bytes 0-3 = 0x0000004A. Appears as the first group of the serial's firmware version.";
CM_ BO_ 2230924544 "[confirmed] Reply to 02213500. 0x000000EB = group 2 of <ECU Info> <Hardware Version>, verbatim.";
CM_ SG_ 2230924544 HardwareVersion "[confirmed] Bytes 0-3 = 0x000000EB. Appears as the second group of the serial's hardware version.";
CM_ BO_ 2230924545 "[confirmed] Reply to 02213501. 0x00000004 = group 2 of <ECU Info> <Firmware Version>, verbatim.";
CM_ SG_ 2230924545 FirmwareVersion "[confirmed] Bytes 0-3 = 0x00000004. Appears as the second group of the serial's firmware version.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567040 "[confirmed] Reply to 02294500. 0x0500 = group 3 of <ECU Info> <Hardware Version>, which
the report writes as 05000000 -- these two bytes zero-extended. AA=45 is the
BMS: 05124502 at the same address carries its model string.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567040 HardwareVersion "[confirmed] Bytes 0-1 = 0x0500, the leading half of the serial's third hardware group.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567041 "[confirmed] Reply to 02294501. 0x0103 = group 3 of <ECU Info> <Firmware Version>, written 01030000 in the report.";
CM_ SG_ 2232567041 FirmwareVersion "[confirmed] Bytes 0-1 = 0x0103, the leading half of the serial's third firmware group.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567042 "[confirmed] The battery pack's model string in ASCII: 5250313353333541 is \"RP13S35A\".
Reply to 02294502, at the BMS identity address.

No signals are defined -- DBC has no string type and splitting it into eight
one-byte integers would make it less readable, not more. The value is the
finding.

WHY IT IS WORTH HAVING. \"13S\" is 13 cells in series, which independently
corroborates the cell count that +RESP:GTBMI reports and that
05FF4602-05FF4605 supply. It also confirms AA=45 as the battery's identity
channel, which is the keystone of the AA scheme in nodes.md: 45 is identity
and 46 is live data for the same component, so 15/16 and 35/36 are read the
same way.";
CM_ BO_ 2232567043 "[hypothesis] Reply to 02294503, unchanged in every session: 00150C0900000C62.

Bytes 1-3 are 0x15 0x0C 0x09 = 21, 12, 9, which reads as a manufacture date of
9 December 2021, and bytes 6-7 are 0x0C62 = 3170, which would be a serial
number within that batch. Both are guesses from shape alone: nothing in the
corpus varies, and there is one pack.

What would test it: read this frame from a second pack. A different date and a
different last word, with the same layout, would settle it; anything else
would not.";
CM_ BO_ 2231317504 "[confirmed] A 10-second uptime tick. Bytes 0-1 and 4-7 are always zero; bytes 2-3 rise
monotonically and never repeat, so every frame of this message is unique.";
CM_ SG_ 2231317504 Uptime "[confirmed] CONFIRMED -- the rate. In the 4 September corpus the field's change between
consecutive frames equalled the elapsed wall-clock time divided by 10, exactly,
at all 76 steps, including across a 2520 s gap that advanced it by 252. The
7 September session reproduced this independently: 706 steps, every delta
exactly 1, against 7060 s elapsed. Wall-clock time is the ground truth, so the
scale needs no further test.

CONFIRMED -- that it is uptime, not an odometer. The field read 2878 when the
4 September session ended and 509 when the 7 September session began, so it
resets. A power-cycle test was recorded in signals.toml as the way to tell
uptime from an odometer, and this is that test passing.

CONFIRMED -- wider than 10 bits. discover.candidate_fields() reports only the
low 10 bits as active within one session, but the 7 September session ran the
field from 509 to 1215, straight past the 1023 where a 10-bit field would wrap,
monotonically and with every delta 1.

CONFIRMED -- that it keeps counting with the recorder switched off. The three
7 September sessions are one continuous run of this counter, to the exact
tick: it ends session 1 at 1215 and begins session 2 at 1389, and the wall
clock gap is 1740 s = 174 ticks; it ends session 2 at 1425 and begins session
3 at 1489, gap 640 s = 64 ticks. Both exact. So the count is tied to elapsed
time and not to anything the recorder does, and those three sessions are one
unbroken 10,070 s of bike uptime with no power cycle in between -- which is
what makes them comparable with each other.

A fourth gap reproduces it after the pack removals reset the count. The
reboot session ends at 124 and the max-speed session begins at 226, and the
gap between those two frames is 1020.0 s = 102 ticks. Exact again, and this
one matters more than the first three: the recorder was not merely switched
off across it, the bike was left alone for 17 minutes with nothing observing
it at all, and the counter still arrived at the value elapsed time predicts.

CONFIRMED -- that an IoT reboot does not reset it. Only pack power-on does.
The reboot session issued three REBOOT commands, all three acknowledged by
+ACK:GTRTO and all three corroborated by a +RESP:GTPNA / +RESP:GTPFA pair,
and across all of them this counter ran 48 -> 124 with every delta exactly 1
and no reset. So the reset condition established from the battery-removal
session is specific: the pack, not the IoT module, and not the ECU coming
back up. This is what separates the two readings of \"power-cycle the bike\".

RESOLVED -- which component sends it, as far as the address scheme goes. It is
AA=34, and 04FF3604 (the 30 ms message) and 02203606 (the headlight bit) are
AA=36 and AA=35's neighbours in the same component group. See nodes.md: AA
reads as <component><channel>, and channel 4 is a 10 s counter. This is the
only one of the three such counters that runs while the bus sleeps, so
whatever component 3 is, it stays powered through sleep. Three of the other
identifiers at that component are the fastest and the slowest messages on the
bus, which is what a controller looks like.

CORRECTION -- it is not powered permanently, only through sleep. That earlier
wording is disproved by the 7 September battery-removal session: with the pack
out, this identifier stops with everything else. Component 3 is supplied from
the pack.

UNKNOWN -- whether it is 16 or 32 bits. Bytes 0-1 are always zero, so they
could be the high half of a 32-bit value. Separating the two needs more than
7.6 days of continuous uptime, which is when a 16-bit field would wrap. The
longest continuous run recorded is 15,160 s.

RESOLVED -- what event starts the count: pack power-on. The 7 September
battery-removal session pulled the pack four times, and the counter behaved
identically every time: it read 2282 before the first removal, and after each
of the four restores its first frame arrived +10.009, +9.992, +10.010 and
+10.011 s later carrying 0. Four resets out of four, each exactly one tick
after power returned. This also settles the earlier drop from 2878 to 509
between 4 and 7 September: the pack had been off the bike.

That makes this counter volatile, which separates it from 02F82400. Over the
same four sessions 02F82400 ran 965 -> 969 -> 971 -> 980 -> 981 -> 987 -> 992
-> 994 without a single reset, including across the four pack removals. Two
counters, two components, one held in volatile memory and one not.";
CM_ BO_ 2197300224 "[confirmed] A second 10-second counter, at AA=24, and NOT the same quantity as
04FF3400. This one counts only the time the bus spends awake.

Proved by the sleep gaps. Between session 1 and session 2 the bike slept for
2390 s and this counter advanced by 2; between sessions 2 and 3 it slept 947 s
and it advanced by 1. Over the same two gaps 04FF3400 advanced by 174 and 64,
exactly the wall clock. Within a session the two run at the same rate, so the
difference is not the scale: this counter stops when the bus does.

It also skips correctly across the short sleep inside session 2. The bus was
down from 113.6 s to 127.0 s, and the counter advanced 1 tick over the 20 s of
wall clock that straddles it -- about 7 s of which the bus was awake.

Cumulative total when first recorded: 965 ticks, so this bike has spent about
2.7 hours awake since whatever last reset the field.

03FF1400 is the same quantity at another address. See nodes.md for the three
semantics side by side. 05FF4400 used to be listed with them and is not a
clock at all; see BmsSequence_4400.

It is also the non-volatile one. It ran 965 -> 969 -> 971 -> 980 -> 981 -> 987
-> 992 -> 994 across the four sessions without a single reset, and the last
step spans the four pack removals of the battery-removal session. 04FF3400
resets at every one of them.";
CM_ SG_ 2197300224 AwakeTime "[confirmed] Bytes 0-1, LITTLE-endian, 10 s per count. C503 reads as 0x03C5 = 965, and the
five frames of session 1 run 965, 966, 967, 968, 969 at 10 s apart. Read
big-endian the sequence would be 0xC503, 0xC603, ... jumping by 256 a step,
which no clock does. The three sessions run 965-969, 971-980, 981-987
continuously.

Every one of the 22 steps recorded is exactly +1. Bytes 2-7 are always zero.

Note the endianness differs from 04FF3400, which is big-endian. Two counters,
two conventions, on the same bus.";
CM_ BO_ 2214532096 "[confirmed] Cumulative awake time again, at AA=14, big-endian and 32 bits wide. Same
quantity and same rate as 02F82400, different origin: it read 0 at the start
of the 7 September corpus and 16 at the end, so it had been reset shortly
before, where 02F82400 was already at 965.

Advanced by 1 over the 2402 s sleep between sessions 1 and 2 and by 1 over the
961 s sleep between 2 and 3, against 240 and 96 ticks of wall clock. So it too
counts awake time and not elapsed time.";
CM_ SG_ 2214532096 AwakeTime "[confirmed] Bytes 0-3 big-endian, 10 s per count. 0x0000000A = 10. Runs 0-3 in session 1,
4-11 in session 2, 12-16 in session 3, every step exactly +1, and skips the
short sleep inside session 2 exactly as 02F82400 does.";
CM_ BO_ 2248098816 "[probable] A counter the BMS increments once per frame of this message. NOT a clock.

CORRECTION. This was recorded as \"BmsAwakeTime_4400\", a fourth 10-second
counter that reset after a long sleep, and the whole reading was wrong. It
survived three sessions because this message is usually sent about every
10.5 s, so the count and the wall clock advanced together by coincidence.

The 7 September battery-removal session broke the coincidence. There the
transmit intervals are 3.1, 68.3, 4.2, 42.8, 4.1, 35.9, 4.1, 27.7, 2.7, 10.5
and 10.5 s, and the value steps by exactly +1 at every one of them. A 10 s
tick cannot advance by 1 in 2.7 s, and cannot advance by only 1 across 68.3 s.

What is true instead, over the whole corpus: the value equals the frame's
index within the session, 0, 1, 2, ..., in all 37 frames of all four
sessions, with no exception and no gap.

CONSEQUENCE -- the deep-sleep threshold experiment recorded here is void. It
was going to lock the bike, wait a measured interval, unlock, and read this
field as elapsed time. It is a frame index, so it would have measured nothing.
The 51 s / 947 s bracket that came from it should not be relied on.

It stays \"probable\" rather than \"confirmed\" because \"counts frames\" and
\"counts an event that happens once per frame\" cannot be told apart from the
recordings. What is confirmed is the negative: it does not measure time.";
CM_ SG_ 2248098816 Sequence "[probable] Bytes 0-3 big-endian. Value == frame index in 37 of 37 frames across four
sessions: 0-4, 0-10, 0-8 and 0-11.

It did not reset at any of the four pack removals in the battery-removal
session, while every counter on the bike itself did. That is expected and is
worth keeping: the BMS travels inside the pack, so pulling the pack off the
bike never interrupts its supply. It is independent support for AA=44/45/46
being inside the battery.";
CM_ BO_ 2197759490 "[probable] Eight bytes of high-entropy data, fresh every time, every 10 s while the bike
is awake. Every frame recorded is unique:

  1DF37DBD1F0BFC27   5B3BA3150375D83B   5D730D0D3E702E5F
  A243CE652C7E6F53   DAF3C66533E81ED7   3D13C50D00ED4D2B

No signals are defined and none should be: a value that is different every
time carries no field to fit. Writing it down is the finding.

WHY THIS LOOKS LIKE AUTHENTICATION rather than noise. It arrives 30 ms after
each tick of 02F82400, the awake-time counter, in every instance -- a counter
paired with an unpredictable value is the shape of a rolling code, and a
counter is exactly what stops one being replayed. The same address, AA=26,
carries the challenge that MEULK produces (02472602).

Bytes 0-7 all vary, entropy 2.5 bits per byte over 7 frames, which is as high
as 7 samples can show. Nothing in it correlates with anything measured.

TREAT WITH CARE. If this is an immobiliser handshake then replaying or forging
it is the interesting part, and this project does not transmit. Never put a
transmitter on this identifier as a flag on the capture path.";
CM_ BO_ 2185700866 "[confirmed] A single frame of eight high-entropy bytes, sent once per MEULK command.

Twice recorded, twice different: C2D31EB512687FF3 on 7 September and
52539E5502CBE49B on 4 September. Same address family as the 10 s nonce
(AA=26), unique TT=47.

It opens a fixed five-second exchange that is byte-for-byte identical in both
corpora apart from the challenge itself:

  02472602  <8 random bytes>   x1
  08122602  0100000000000000   x4   first reply 0.07 s later
  02407600  0100000000000000   x5
  08F97603  0101000000000000   x6

The sequence runs with the bus otherwise ASLEEP -- in the 7 September session
it happened 27 s after LOCK -- so whatever it releases does not need the ECU
awake.

CONFIRMED -- this exchange releases the battery. The battery-removal session
issued MEULK four times and the pack came off the bike four times. Each time,
the exchange ran and then the ENTIRE BUS went silent, heartbeat included, at
+2.98, +3.00, +3.11 and +3.04 s after the +ACK:GTRTO. The serial console kept
running on its own backup cell throughout and reported main_power_v = 0.000 in
every blackout against 53.5 V outside them, so pack power really was gone and
not merely quiet.

There is no unexplained blackout in the session and no MEULK without one.

The 3.0 s is not operator reaction time: it varies by 0.07 s over four trials,
which no hand achieves, and it falls at the end of the CAN exchange rather than
at a fixed offset from the command. The disconnection follows the handshake.
What is NOT established is the mechanism -- whether the pack contactor opens
and the operator then lifts a dead pack out, or the latch releases and the
contacts break as the pack moves. Both fit. Telling them apart needs the pack
released and left in place.

Those four identifiers plus the five that survive a sleeping bus plus the four
light identifiers are exactly the 13 in the 4 September corpus, which accounts
for that recording completely.";
CM_ BO_ 2231318020 "[hypothesis] The fastest message on the bus at 30 ms, and the most valuable unknown. Not
decoded, but enough is now known to rule things out and to say what to record
next.

Layout, identical in all three 7 September sessions (1308, 2703 and 1881
frames):

  byte 0  always 0        byte 4  always 0
  byte 1  1 to 9          byte 5  200, or 0 for the first second after waking
  byte 2  always 0        byte 6  0 or 1, in brief bursts around a wake
  byte 3  2 to 4          byte 7  always 0

So bytes 0-1 and 2-3 are two 16-bit values, both far below 256, and bytes 4-5
hold the constant 200.

NOT A LAMP. Byte 1 has mean 4.47 and byte 3 mean 3.05, both fluctuating every
30 ms with no correlation between them (r = 0.07). That is two analogue inputs
idling at a small offset, sampled at 33 Hz. A lamp does not need 33 Hz and
does not need two noisy channels. Whatever the address AA=36 means, this is
not the headlight's own traffic.

WHAT IT IS PROBABLY. Two ADC channels reading near zero on a stationary bike,
with 200 as a fixed limit or scale. The torque sensor, the throttle and the
motor current are all in that shape and all read zero here. The bike has never
been ridden, so a channel that is zero when stationary cannot be told from a
channel that is broken or from an offset.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT, in order of cost:
  1. Ride it. Any motion at all separates speed and torque from everything
     that stays flat, and this message will move first because it is fastest.
  2. Turn the throttle and squeeze each brake on the bench with the bike
     unlocked. Those move one channel at a time without the bike moving.
  3. Watch byte 5. It sits at 0 for the first second after each wake and then
     goes to 200 and stays. If 200 is a limit, riding should not change it.

USE THIS MESSAGE AS THE WAKE DETECTOR. Its active runs bound the awake window
better than anything else on the bus: they begin 2.1-2.6 s after each UNLOCK
and end 0.3-0.8 s after each LOCK, over four unlocks and three locks. That is
how the lock latency asymmetry was shown to be the bus waking rather than a
latch -- see 13B76400.Unlocked.";
CM_ SG_ 2231318020 Constant200 "[hypothesis] Bytes 4-5. Holds 200 (0xC8) in all but the first second after a wake, where it
is 0, and in a 90 ms blip during a re-unlock. Named for what it is rather than
what it might mean. A limit, a scale or a commanded setpoint would all look
like this; only riding will tell them apart.";
CM_ SG_ 2231318020 AnalogueB "[hypothesis] Bytes 2-3. Stationary value 2 to 4, mean 3.05, uncorrelated with AnalogueA
(r = 0.07) -- which is the reason to believe they are two separate channels
rather than one 32-bit value.";
CM_ SG_ 2231318020 AnalogueA "[hypothesis] Bytes 0-1. Byte 0 has been zero in all 5892 frames recorded, so the width is a
guess; 16 bits is written because bytes 2-3 clearly pair the same way.
Stationary value 1 to 9, mean 4.47, resampling every 30 ms. Unnamed on purpose:
calling it Torque or Throttle would record a guess as a fact.";
CM_ BO_ 2214532611 "[hypothesis] 105 ms, and constant for a whole session, which is a strange pair. Bytes 0-3
big-endian read as a value very close to the pack voltage:

  session 1   0x0000D11F = 53535       pack was 53620-53631 mV
  session 2   0x0000D10E = 53518       pack was 53580-53592 mV
  session 3   0x0000D0DC = 53468       pack was 53562-53576 mV

It falls between sessions in the same order as the pack does, and sits 90 to
110 mV below it. But 05FF4610's pack voltage moves by about 12 mV within a
session while this does not move at all, so it cannot be a live measurement of
the same thing. A value latched when the bus wakes would look exactly like
this.

Worth having because of where it is: AA=16 is the address that carries the
rear light bit, and a component that reports a pack voltage is not a rear
lamp. That is part of the argument in nodes.md that AA names a controller
rather than a light.

WHAT WOULD DISPROVE \"latched at wake\": unlock, wait for the value, then load
the pack hard enough to move 05FF4610 by more than 100 mV, and see whether
this follows. If it does, it is a filtered live reading and the constancy was
only a symptom of a very quiet bench.";
CM_ SG_ 2214532611 VoltageLike "[hypothesis] Bytes 0-3 big-endian. Three values, one per session, all within 0.2 % of the
pack voltage at the time. The unit is written as mV to make the comparison
easy to run; it is a hypothesis, not a measurement, and one value per session
is three data points.";
CM_ BO_ 2214531072 "[probable] A heartbeat. Every 105 ms, payload 0840112200000001. It runs while the bus
sleeps, so it is one of the five identifiers a locked bike still sends. Node
field 10->00 appears nowhere else.

CORRECTION -- it is not invariant. This was recorded as \"never varies\",
byte-identical across 80,872 frames of four sessions. That was true of every
recording made up to then and it was still the wrong conclusion, because not
one of those recordings contained a cold boot. The battery-removal session
supplied four, and in each of them byte 0 is 0x00 for the first two seconds
after power returns and 0x08 for ever after. See SystemReady below.

The remaining seven bytes have never varied, across 83,259 frames.

It stays \"probable\" rather than confirmed because nothing has tied it to a
component or an action. Calling it a heartbeat is an inference from its shape.";
CM_ SG_ 2214531072 SystemReady "[probable] Bit 0x08 of byte 0. Clear for the first seconds after the pack is connected,
then set, and it never clears again while power is held.

Four pack removals in the 7 September battery-removal session, four identical
boots. The heartbeat itself restarts +0.10 to +0.12 s after power returns,
already carrying 0x00; the bit sets at +2.01, +2.10, +2.22 and +1.70 s. Every
one of the 711 frames recorded before the first removal has it set, as does
every frame of the three earlier sessions and the 4 September corpus.

Reading it as \"initialised\" is an inference from the timing, not a
measurement, which is why this is \"probable\". Two facts support it: the delay
is close to the 2.1-2.6 s that 04FF3604 takes to start after an UNLOCK, and
04FF3400 issues its first tick 10 s after the same instant.

WHAT WOULD DISPROVE IT: a recording where the bit clears without a power
interruption -- during a ride, a fault, or a lock/unlock. That would make it a
status bit rather than a boot flag. Nothing in the corpus shows this, but the
corpus contains only four boots.";
